//? Title: Mixed Precision: Rounding, Loss Scaling and Skipped Updates
//? Subtitle: Float32 master weights with explicitly emulated low-precision storage
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
We round values to binary16 and bfloat16 representable sets while retaining Float32
storage and arithmetic on the CPU. Three experiments expose representational limits,
recover a tiny rounded gradient through loss scaling, and compare small dense fits.
A deliberately overflowing gradient checks that skipping an update preserves weights,
Adam moments and its update counter. These experiments make no claim about GPU speed,
native low-precision memory use, or bitwise agreement with a hardware mixed-precision kernel.
)
\toc

> The method


Binary16 has five exponent bits and ten stored fraction bits: its smallest positive
normal is 2^-14, smallest positive subnormal 2^-24 and largest finite value 65504.
Bfloat16 keeps Float32's eight exponent bits but only seven fraction bits. Its range
is wider than binary16's, while spacing near one is coarser: 2^-7 versus 2^-10.
Rounding uses nearest with ties to even. Binary16 subnormals are retained here.

The native emulation rounds dense-layer inputs, master weights, matmul outputs,
biases and bias-add outputs. The multiply/accumulate itself still uses Float32.
Its custom autograd VJP rounds the incoming gradient to the selected format. This
is a storage-emulation surrogate, not the derivative of piecewise-constant rounding.

For a positive loss multiplier S, the intended scaling identity is

$$
 g=\nabla L=\frac{\nabla(SL)}{S}.
$$
\alttext(The unscaled gradient is the gradient of the scaled loss divided by its positive multiplier.)

Scaling can move small gradients into the representable range before the surrogate
rounding edge. Divide into independent Float32 buffers and check finiteness before
calling the optimizer. Overflow causes a skipped update and scale backoff; successful
updates eventually grow the bounded scale. The host owns this control flow.


The concept diagram is generated from the retained local TikZ source. LaTeX with
PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm, is needed only to regenerate it.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/mixed_precision_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/mixed_precision_concept.tikz > build/mixed_precision.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/mixed_precision_concept.svg build/mixed_precision_concept.dvi > build/mixed_precision.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/mixed_precision_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
