> Simple examples

>> Range and spacing are different constraints

The table includes a tiny derivative, the subnormal boundary, a midpoint near one,
and overflow. Both reduced formats have coarser spacing than Float32. Bfloat16 keeps
1e-8 nonzero; binary16 rounds it to zero. At 65520, binary16 rounds to infinity.
The plot uses only the finite values near one so that infinity is never drawn as an
ordinary finite point.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mixed_rounding.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::nn;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  std::vector<double>
      x = {1e-8, std::ldexp(1., -24), 1., 1.00048828125, 1.001, 1.00390625, 65504., 65520.},
      h, b;
  for (double a : x) {
    h.push_back(round_float16(float(a)));
    b.push_back(round_bfloat16(float(a)));
  }
  t.add_column("input", x);
  t.add_column("binary16", h);
  t.add_column("bfloat16", b);
  std::vector<double> near, half, bfloat;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 128; ++i) {
    const float a = 1 + i / 8192.f;
    near.push_back(a);
    half.push_back(round_float16(a));
    bfloat.push_back(round_bfloat16(a));
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(near, near, "identity", {22, 163, 74})
      .line(near, half, "binary16", {37, 99, 235})
      .line(near, bfloat, "bfloat16", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Representable spacing near one")
      .x_label("Float32 input")
      .y_label("rounded value")
      .y_limits(.999, 1.021);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(950, 460).save_svg("build/mixed_rounding.svg");
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md(9));
}
```
