> A small dense model

>> Master weights stay Float32 and overflow skips Adam

Sixteen deterministic two-feature observations follow y=.73*x0-1.27*x1+.13.
All three modes start with seed 11, use Adam at rate .03 and apply 400 full-batch
updates. Parameter creation happens on the first forward pass, before constructing
the optimizer. Only binary16 uses dynamic scaling. After its first successful update,
a separate scale of 1e12 deliberately overflows. We compare every master weight,
optimizer slot and counter before and after that skipped attempt. Ordinary finite
training then continues. This is one tiny deterministic regression, not a benchmark
or evidence that any precision is universally accurate enough.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mixed_fit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::nn;
  set_num_threads(2);
  std::vector<float> xv(32), yv(16);
  for (int i = 0; i < 16; ++i) {
    float a = std::sin(i * .7f), b = std::cos(i * .4f);
    xv[2 * i]     = a;
    xv[2 * i + 1] = b;
    yv[i]         = .73f * a - 1.27f * b + .13f;
  }
  const auto               x = from_vector(xv, {16, 2}), y = from_vector(yv, {16, 1});
  auto                     p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame   table;
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::vector<double>      losses, skips, scale_end;
  int                      mode = 0;
  for (auto precision :
       {PrecisionEmulation::Float32, PrecisionEmulation::Float16, PrecisionEmulation::BFloat16}) {
    ParameterStore store(11);
    auto loss = [&] { return mean(square(mixed_dense(store, "dense", x, 2, 1, precision) - y)); };
    (void)loss();
    OptimizerConfig c;
    c.kind = OptimizerKind::Adam;
    Optimizer        opt(store, .03, c);
    DynamicLossScale scale(
        precision == PrecisionEmulation::Float16 ? 128 : 1,
        100,
        1,
        precision == PrecisionEmulation::Float16 ? 65536 : 1);
    std::vector<double> steps = {0}, mse = {std::log10(loss().item())};
    int                 applied = 0, skipped = 0;
    bool                forced   = false;
    auto                snapshot = [&] {
      std::vector<std::vector<double>> v;
      for (const auto& t : store.parameters())
        v.push_back(t.to_vector<double>());
      for (const auto& [name, t] : opt.state())
        v.push_back(t.to_vector<double>());
      return v;
    };
    for (int attempt = 0; applied < 400 && attempt < 450; ++attempt) {
      std::vector<Tensor> g;
      if (!unscale_gradients(grad(loss() * scale.value(), store.parameters()), scale.value(), &g)) {
        scale.overflow();
        ++skipped;
        continue;
      }
      opt.step(g);
      scale.applied();
      ++applied;
      if (applied % 10 == 0) {
        steps.push_back(applied);
        mse.push_back(std::log10(std::max(1e-16, loss().item())));
      }
      if (precision == PrecisionEmulation::Float16 && !forced) {
        const auto before = snapshot();
        if (unscale_gradients(grad(loss() * 1e12, store.parameters()), 1e12, &g))
          throw std::runtime_error("forced overflow absent");
        if (before != snapshot() || opt.iterations() != 1)
          throw std::runtime_error("skipped attempt mutated Adam");
        forced = true;
        std::print("Forced overflow: all weights, moments and counter unchanged.\n\n");
      }
    }
    if (applied != 400 || opt.iterations() != 400 || loss().item() >= 3e-4)
      throw std::runtime_error("fit reference failed");
    names.push_back(precision_name(precision));
    losses.push_back(loss().item());
    skips.push_back(skipped);
    scale_end.push_back(scale.value());
    const dm::graphics::RGB color = mode == 0   ? dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}
                                    : mode == 1 ? dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}
                                                : dm::graphics::RGB{22, 163, 74};
    p.line(steps, mse, precision_name(precision), color);
    ++mode;
  }
  table.add_column("storage_emulation", names);
  table.add_column("final_mse", losses);
  table.add_column("ordinary_overflow_skips", skips);
  table.add_column("final_loss_scale", scale_end);
  p.title("One deterministic dense regression")
      .x_label("applied Adam updates")
      .y_label("log10 full-data MSE")
      .y_limits(-16, 2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(950, 460).save_svg("build/mixed_fit.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\nEach mode applied 400 updates; all master weights remained Float32.\n",
      table.to_md(10));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Forced overflow: all weights, moments and counter unchanged.

| storage_emulation | final_mse | ordinary_overflow_skips | final_loss_scale |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| float32 | 0.000000000 | 0 | 1 |
| float16 | 0.000000105 | 0 | 2048 |
| bfloat16 | 0.000014165 | 0 | 1 |


Each mode applied 400 updates; all master weights remained Float32.
\image(figures/mixed_fit.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Three emulated storage choices fit the same toy data; a forced overflow independently checks the skip policy.)
\alttext(Three log10 MSE curves fall over 400 applied Adam updates. A table records final losses, ordinary skipped attempts and loss scales. Printed evidence checks that one forced overflow leaves all Adam state unchanged.)

> Conclusion

The experiment separates range, spacing, loss scaling and optimizer state. Reduced
formats use Float32 carriers and arithmetic; their rounded VJPs model selected storage
edges. Loss scaling recovers a small derivative here, and nonfinite gradients cause no
optimizer call. Exact resume of a dynamic-scale run must also retain its host scale
and successful-update history, separately from model/optimizer checkpoints.

> References

- TensorFlow authors. [Mixed precision guide|https://www.tensorflow.org/guide/mixed_precision]. Float32 master variables and loss-scaling motivation; this report uses explicit CPU storage emulation rather than native hardware kernels.
- Kingma, D. P. and Ba, J. (2015). [Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization|https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980]. Moment state and update counting.
